<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Assignment 7: Functions of Several Variables, Limits and Continuity, and
  Partial Derivatives.

  The first four problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for Functions of
  Several Variables" (pp-26, pp-27, rw17-6 and pp-25), split out into an
  assignment at the instructor's request, followed by the odd-numbered
  problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for Limits and Continuity in
  Higher Dimensions" (rm2-lim-1-a, rm2-lim-1-c, rm2-lim-3, rw23-2, rw21-6
  and rw22-6), followed by the first three problems of the old worksheet
  "Exercises for Partial Derivatives" (rm2-pd-1, pp-28 and q1-3).  The
  remaining problems of all three worksheets went to review-problems-3.ptx.

  rw17-6 once had a second part asking for the normal line to the level
  surface; it was removed at the instructor's request before the split.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-7" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 7</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems sketch domains of functions of two variables, reason about
level curves, identify and sketch level surfaces, and ask when a limit of a
function of two variables exists and how to show that one does not, and
compute and estimate higher order partial derivatives. If you would like to
review the material first, see
<xref ref="chapter-functions-several-variables" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="chapter-limits-continuity" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="chapter-partial-derivatives" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="pp-26">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the domain of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{y - 3x}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The square root is defined exactly when its argument is non-negative,
so the domain is
<md>
\{(x,y) : y - 3x \ge 0\} = \{(x,y) : y \ge 3x\},
</md>
the closed half-plane on and above the line <m>y = 3x</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-domain"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-domain">
<caption>
The domain of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{y-3x}</m> is the shaded closed
half-plane <m>y \ge 3x</m>, including the boundary line itself.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-domain">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-6.6,3.4,6.6)">
<polygon at="dm-region" points="((-2,-6),(-3.2,-6),(-3.2,6.4),(2.13,6.4))"
         fill="#56B4E9" fill-opacity="0.35" stroke="none"/>
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="dm-line" endpoints="((-2.13,-6.4),(2.13,6.4))"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="dm-line-lab" anchor="(2.05,5.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=3x</m>
</label>
<label at="dm-reg-lab" anchor="(-1.9,2.0)" alignment="w">
<m>y\ge 3x</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A steep blue line of slope three through the origin, y equals three x. Everything on and above and to the left of the line is shaded light blue; this shaded closed half plane is the domain.">
  <annotation ref="dm-line" text="The boundary line y equals three x, which belongs to the domain."/>
  <annotation ref="dm-region" text="The shaded closed half plane y greater than or equal to three x, the domain of the function."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-27">
<statement>
<p>
In <xref ref="fig-pp-bad-contours"/> are level curves of
<m>g(x,y) = 2</m> and <m>g(x,y) = 3</m> for some function
<m>g(x,y)</m>. What's wrong?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-bad-contours">
<caption>
Two curves labelled <m>g = 2</m> and <m>g = 3</m> that cross each
other.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-bad-contours">
<diagram dimensions="(300,190)" margins="12">
<definition>d1(t) = (t, 0.55*t + 0.15*sin(2*t))</definition>
<definition>d2(t) = (t, 2.2 - 0.55*t - 0.15*sin(2*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.3,-0.35,4.3,2.6)">
<parametric-curve at="bc-1" function="d1" domain="(0,4)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="bc-2" function="d2" domain="(0,4)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="bc-P" p="(2.269,1.1)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="bc-P-lab" anchor="(2.32,1.0)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-l1" anchor="(-0.25,0.05)" alignment="e">
<m>g=2</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-l2" anchor="(-0.25,2.15)" alignment="e">
<m>g=3</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-r1" anchor="(4.1,2.15)" alignment="w">
<m>g=2</m>
</label>
<label at="bc-r2" anchor="(4.1,0.05)" alignment="w">
<m>g=3</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two curves cross in the middle of the picture at a point P. Reading the labels, one curve is marked g equals two at its left end and g equals two at its right end after crossing, while the other is marked g equals three at both of its ends, so the two different level curves intersect.">
  <annotation ref="bc-P" text="The point P where the two level curves cross."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Two level curves belonging to <em>different</em> values can never
intersect. At the point <m>P</m> where the drawn curves cross, the
first curve says <m>g(P) = 2</m> while the second says
<m>g(P) = 3</m>. Since <m>g</m> is a function, it assigns exactly one
output to the point <m>P</m>, so <m>2 = 3</m> — which is impossible.
Hence no function <m>g</m> can have the level curves shown.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-6">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>g</m> be a function of 3 variables defined by
<md>
g(x,y,z) = \sqrt{4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2}.
</md>
Find the equation for the level surface of <m>g</m> through the point
<m>\left(2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right)</m> and sketch this level surface.
Label any points where the surface crosses a coordinate axis AND label
the value of <m>g</m> on the surface.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Evaluating <m>g</m> at the given point,
<md>
g\left(2, \sqrt{11}, 1\right)
= \sqrt{4(4) + 11 + 9(1)}
= \sqrt{36} = 6,
</md>
so the level surface is <m>g(x,y,z) = 6</m>, i.e.
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt{4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2} = 6
\amp \;\Rightarrow\;
4x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2 = 36</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{x^2}{9} + \frac{y^2}{36} + \frac{z^2}{4} = 1.</mrow>
</md>
This is an <term>ellipsoid</term> with semi-axes <m>3</m>, <m>6</m>,
and <m>2</m>. It crosses the axes at <m>(\pm 3, 0, 0)</m>,
<m>(0, \pm 6, 0)</m>, and <m>(0, 0, \pm 2)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw17-ellipsoid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw17-ellipsoid">
<caption>
The level surface <m>g = 6</m>, i.e. the ellipsoid
<m>\frac{x^2}{9} + \frac{y^2}{36} + \frac{z^2}{4} = 1</m>, with its
axis intercepts.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
An ellipsoid centered at the origin, elongated along the y axis. It
meets the x axis at plus and minus three, the y axis at plus and minus
six, and the z axis at plus and minus two. Three dashed ellipses show
its cross sections in the coordinate planes.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-1.9) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-3.6,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (1.15,0.82) -- (-1.5,-1.07) node[below left] {$x$};
  % the ellipsoid outline: semi-axis 3 along y (drawn), 1 along z (drawn)
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 1cm);
  % cross-section ellipses
  \draw[mblue, dashed] (0,0) ellipse (0.55cm and 1cm);
  \draw[mblue, dashed] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 0.28cm);
  % intercepts
  \fill (3,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=1pt] {$(0,6,0)$};
  \fill (-3,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$(0,-6,0)$};
  \fill (0,1) circle (1.6pt) node[above right=1pt] {$(0,0,2)$};
  \fill (0,-1) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=1pt] {$(0,0,-2)$};
  \fill (0.55,0.39) circle (1.6pt) node[above right=0pt] {$(3,0,0)$};
  \fill (-0.55,-0.39) circle (1.6pt);
  % label the level value
  \node[mblue] at (2.1,1.35) {$g=6$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-25">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the level surface <m>f(x,y,z) = z^2 - x^2 - y^2</m> for
<m>f(x,y,z) = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The level surface is <m>z^2 - x^2 - y^2 = 4</m>, i.e.
<md>
\frac{z^2}{4} - \frac{x^2}{4} - \frac{y^2}{4} = 1.
</md>
Rewriting as <m>z^2 = 4 + x^2 + y^2</m> shows that
<m>z^2 \ge 4</m>, so <m>z \ge 2</m> or <m>z \le -2</m>: no part of the
surface lies between the two horizontal planes <m>z = \pm 2</m>. The
traces are
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>z = c</m> with <m>|c| \gt 2</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 = c^2 - 4</m>,
circles;
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x = c</m>: <m>z^2 - y^2 = 4 + c^2</m>, hyperbolas;
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = c</m>: <m>z^2 - x^2 = 4 + c^2</m>, hyperbolas.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
Hence the level surface is a <term>hyperboloid of two sheets</term>,
symmetric about the <m>z</m>-axis, with vertices <m>(0,0,\pm 2)</m>.
See <xref ref="fig-pp-level-surface"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-level-surface">
<caption>
The level surface <m>z^2 - x^2 - y^2 = 4</m>: a hyperboloid of two
sheets about the <m>z</m>-axis with vertices <m>(0,0,\pm 2)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<description>
<p>
Two bowl shaped sheets in a three dimensional coordinate system, one
opening upward with its lowest point at zero comma zero comma two, and
its mirror image opening downward with highest point at zero comma
zero comma negative two. Each sheet is capped by an ellipse.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-3.7) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.9,0.65) -- (-1.25,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
  % upper sheet: x = +-0.85*sqrt(z^2-4) for z from 2 to 3.4
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, \x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({-0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, \x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,3.4) ellipse (2.25cm and 0.33cm);
  % lower sheet
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, -\x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.4, samples=31]
    ({-0.85*sqrt(\x*\x - 4)}, -\x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,-3.4) ellipse (2.25cm and 0.33cm);
  % vertices
  \fill (0,2) circle (1.6pt) node[right=3pt] {$(0,0,2)$};
  \fill (0,-2) circle (1.6pt) node[right=3pt] {$(0,0,-2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-lim-1-a">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>\lim\limits_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> exists, the function
<m>f(x,y)</m> must be defined or continuous at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
We can for example define <m>f(x,y)</m> to be zero
when <m>(x,y) \ne (0,0)</m> and to be <m>1</m> when
<m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>. If we then pick <m>(a,b) = (0,0)</m>, the limit
of <m>f(x,y)</m> for <m>(x,y) \to (a,b)</m> exists but it is not equal
to <m>f(a,b)</m>. We could also leave <m>f(x,y)</m> undefined at the
origin to give another counterexample.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-lim-1-c">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>f(x,y) \to L</m> as <m>(x,y) \to (a,b)</m> along any line
through <m>(a,b)</m>, then
<m>\lim\limits_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y) = L</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
A first counterexample is the function of <xref ref="example-lim-curved-path"/>,
<md>
f(x,y) = \frac{(x-1)^2(y-2)^3}{(x-1)^4+(y-2)^6}, \qquad (a,b) = (1,2).
</md>
Write <m>u = x-1</m> and <m>v = y-2</m>. Along the vertical line
<m>x = 1</m> we have <m>u = 0</m>, so <m>f(x,y) = 0</m>. Every other
line through <m>(1,2)</m> is <m>v = mu</m>, and along it
<md>
f(x,y) = \frac{m^3 u^5}{u^4 + m^6 u^6} = \frac{m^3 u}{1 + m^6 u^2} \to 0
\quad\text{as } u \to 0.
</md>
So <m>f(x,y) \to 0</m> along every line through <m>(1,2)</m>, and the
hypothesis holds with <m>L = 0</m>. But along the curve
<m>(x-1)^2 = (y-2)^3</m>, traced as <m>x = 1 + t^3</m>,
<m>y = 2 + t^2</m>, we have <m>u = t^3</m>, <m>v = t^2</m> and
<md>
f(x,y) = \frac{t^6\cdot t^6}{t^{12} + t^{12}} = \frac12
\quad\text{for every } t \ne 0,
</md>
so <m>f(x,y) \to \frac12</m> along this curve. The two values differ,
so <m>\lim\limits_{(x,y)\to(1,2)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist; in
particular it is not <m>L = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
A second counterexample is
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
0 \amp \text{if } y \le 0 \text{ or } y \ge 2x^2 \\
1 \amp \text{if } 0 \lt y \lt 2x^2
\end{cases}
</md>
Along every straight line passing through the origin this will be
constant <m>0</m> near the origin, but the limit approaching via the
parabola <m>y = x^2</m> is <m>1</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rm2-lines-counterexample"/>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-rm2-lines-counterexample">
<caption>
The counterexample. The function is <m>1</m> on the shaded
region <m>0 \lt y \lt 2x^2</m> and <m>0</m> everywhere else. Every
line through the origin leaves the shaded region near the origin, but
the parabola <m>y = x^2</m> stays inside it.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rm2-lines">
<diagram dimensions="(380,260)" margins="12">
<definition>ptop(t) = (t, 2*t^2)</definition>
<definition>pmid(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
<definition>ftop(t) = 2*t^2</definition>
<definition>fzero(t) = 0</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-0.5,1.5,1.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<area-between-curves at="rm2-region" domain="(-0.95,0.95)"
      function1="ftop" function2="fzero"
      fill="#E69F00" fill-opacity="0.30" stroke="none"/>
<parametric-curve at="rm2-ptop" function="ptop" domain="(-0.95,0.95)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="rm2-pmid" function="pmid" domain="(-1.3,1.3)"
                  stroke="#8B1A4A" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="rm2-line" endpoints="((-1.45,-0.44),(1.45,0.44))"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<point at="rm2-origin" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<label at="rm2-ptop-lab" anchor="(0.72,1.55)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=2x^2</m>
</label>
<label at="rm2-pmid-lab" anchor="(-1.28,1.55)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>y=x^2</m>
</label>
<label at="rm2-line-lab" anchor="(-1.42,-0.30)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
<m>y=mx</m>
</label>
<label at="rm2-one-lab" anchor="(0.62,0.35)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=1</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The region between the x axis and the parabola y equals two x squared is shaded orange; the function equals one there and zero everywhere else. A dashed dark red parabola y equals x squared runs through the middle of the shaded region. A straight blue line through the origin with small positive slope lies above the shaded region near the origin.">
  <annotation ref="rm2-region" text="The region zero less than y less than two x squared, where the function equals one."/>
  <annotation ref="rm2-pmid" text="The parabola y equals x squared, which approaches the origin inside the region, so the limit along it is one."/>
  <annotation ref="rm2-line" text="A typical line through the origin. Near the origin it lies outside the region, so the function is zero along it."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-lim-3">
<statement>
<p>
Discuss continuity of the following functions. At any point where a
function is not continuous, say which of the three conditions in
<xref ref="def-continuity-two-variables"/> are satisfied there and which are not.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{2x^4 y}{x^8 + y^2}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>g(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2\cos^2 x}{x^2 + (y-1)^2}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
This is obviously continuous whenever the denominator is nonzero,
i.e. everywhere except at the origin. But at the origin it is not
continuous (or rather we can't extend the function to the origin in a
way which makes it continuous): if we approach the origin via any of
the coordinate axes we get a limit of <m>0</m>, but if we approach it
via the path <m>y = x^4</m> we get a limit
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{2x^4 x^4}{x^8 + x^8} = 1.
</md>
Hence the limit <m>\lim\limits_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> does not
exist.
</p>
<p>
In terms of the definition: at every point other than the origin all
three conditions hold. At the origin, condition 1 fails, since
<m>f</m> is not defined there (the denominator is <m>0</m>), and
condition 2 fails as well, since the limit does not exist; so condition
3 cannot hold either.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
This is continuous when the denominator is nonzero, so everywhere
except at <m>(0,1)</m>. At <m>(0,1)</m> it can't be defined in a way
which makes the function continuous: approaching <m>(0,1)</m> via the
<m>y</m>-axis, we get the limit
<md>
\lim_{y\to 1} \frac{0\cdot\cos^2(0)}{(y-1)^2} = 0.
</md>
On the other hand, if we approach <m>(0,1)</m> on the line
<m>y = 1</m> we get the limit
<md>
\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^2\cos^2 x}{x^2} = \cos^2(0) = 1.
</md>
Hence the limit <m>\lim\limits_{(x,y)\to(0,1)} g(x,y)</m> does not
exist.
</p>
<p>
In terms of the definition: at every point other than <m>(0,1)</m> all
three conditions hold. At <m>(0,1)</m>, condition 1 fails, since
<m>g</m> is not defined there, and condition 2 fails as well, since the
limit does not exist; so condition 3 cannot hold either.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
It is not necessary to discuss whether <m>f</m> or <m>g</m> can be
made continuous by assigning a value at the point in question: the
problem does not ask for it.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-2">
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate each of the following limits or show that it does not exist.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{xy^5}{x^8 + y^{10}}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x^8 - y^8}{x^4 - y^4}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Along the path <m>x = y^5</m>:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{xy^5}{x^8 + y^{10}}
= \lim_{y\to 0} \frac{y^{10}}{y^{40} + y^{10}}
= \lim_{y\to 0} \frac{1}{y^{30} + 1} = 1.
</md>
Along the path <m>x = 0</m>:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{xy^5}{x^8 + y^{10}}
= \lim_{y\to 0} \frac{0}{0 + y^{10}} = 0.
</md>
Since <m>1 \ne 0</m>, the limit does not exist.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We factor the numerator as a difference of squares:
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x^8 - y^8}{x^4 - y^4}
\amp = \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)}
\frac{\left(x^4 - y^4\right)\left(x^4 + y^4\right)}{x^4 - y^4}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} x^4 + y^4 = 2.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-6">
<statement>
<p>
Find the limit, if it exists, or show that it does not exist:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x^2 + y^2 - 2}{|x-1| + |y-1|}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Along the path <m>x = 1</m>, <m>y \to 1^{+}</m> (so that
<m>|y - 1| = y - 1</m>):
<md>
\lim_{y\to 1^{+}} \frac{y^2 - 1}{y - 1}
= \lim_{y\to 1^{+}} \frac{(y-1)(y+1)}{y-1}
= \lim_{y\to 1^{+}} (y + 1) = 2.
</md>
Along the path <m>x = 1</m>, <m>y \to 1^{-}</m> (so that
<m>|y - 1| = -(y - 1)</m>):
<md>
\lim_{y\to 1^{-}} \frac{y^2 - 1}{-(y - 1)}
= \lim_{y\to 1^{-}} -(y + 1) = -2.
</md>
Since the limits computed along the two paths are not equal, the limit
does not exist.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-6">
<statement>
<p>
Find the following limit, if it exists, or show that it does not
exist:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{y^4}{x^4 + 3y^4}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Along the path <m>x = 0</m>:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{y^4}{x^4 + 3y^4}
= \lim_{y\to 0} \frac{y^4}{3y^4} = \frac13.
</md>
Along the path <m>y = x</m>:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{y^4}{x^4 + 3y^4}
= \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^4}{x^4 + 3x^4} = \frac14.
</md>
Since <m>\frac13 \ne \frac14</m>, the limit does not exist.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Partial Derivatives" ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rm2-pd-1">
<title>Higher order computation</title>
<statement>
<p>
Prove that <m>c(x,t) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}}</m>
is a solution of the diffusion equation
<md>
\frac{\partial c}{\partial t} = D\,\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We compute the needed derivatives:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial c}{\partial t}
\amp = -\frac{1}{2\sqrt{D}\,t^{3/2}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}
+ \frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,\frac{x^2}{4Dt^2}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial c}{\partial x}
\amp = -\frac{x}{2Dt}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}
\amp = -\frac{1}{2Dt\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}
+ \frac{x^2}{4D^2t^2}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We see that the right hand side of the third equation is
<m>\dfrac{1}{D}</m> times the right hand side of the first, i.e.
multiplying <m>\dfrac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}</m> by <m>D</m>
gives exactly <m>\dfrac{\partial c}{\partial t}</m>, showing that
<m>c</m> satisfies the diffusion equation.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-28">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + 3y^2z^3 + \ln(xz)</m>. Compute
<m>\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial z}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note first that <m>\ln(xz) = \ln x + \ln z</m>, which makes the
differentiation easier. Treating the other variables as constants,
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = y + \frac1x
\;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} = -\frac{1}{x^2}.
</md>
For the mixed partial, differentiate first with respect to <m>z</m>
and then with respect to <m>y</m>:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z} = 9y^2z^2 + \frac1z
\;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial z}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(9y^2z^2 + \frac1z\right)
= 18yz^2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1-3">
<statement>
<p>
A metal plate occupies the square <m>0 \le x \le 5</m>,
<m>0 \le y \le 5</m>, and is heated so that the temperature at the
point <m>(x,y)</m> is <m>T(x,y)</m>, measured in degrees Fahrenheit.
Distances are measured in feet. The table below records some measured
values of <m>T</m>.
</p>

<table xml:id="tab-q1-temps">
<title>Measured temperatures <m>T(x,y)</m> in <m>{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="medium" left="medium">
<row bottom="medium">
<cell></cell><cell right="medium"><m>y=1</m></cell><cell><m>y=1.5</m></cell><cell><m>y=2</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>70</m></cell><cell><m>65</m></cell><cell><m>58</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1.5</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>74</m></cell><cell><m>68</m></cell><cell><m>59</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=2</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>79</m></cell><cell><m>75</m></cell><cell><m>65</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_x(1,1)</m> and interpret your answer, with units, in
the context of the problem.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_{xy}(1,1)</m>, and give its units.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
An ant walks across the plate along the path
<md>
x = 1 - \frac{\ln(2t-3)}{8}, \qquad y = \frac{2^t}{4},
\qquad 1.5 \lt t \lt 4 .
</md>
Approximate the rate at which the temperature the ant feels is
changing at the moment it is at the point <m>(x,y) = (1,1)</m>, and
give the units.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> A partial derivative is a limit of difference
quotients, so with the data we have we approximate it by a difference
quotient. Holding <m>y=1</m> fixed and stepping forward in <m>x</m>
from <m>x=1</m> to <m>x=1.5</m>,
<md>
T_x(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1) - T(1,1)}{1.5-1}
         = \frac{74-70}{0.5}
         = 8 .
</md>
The units are degrees Fahrenheit per foot. The interpretation: at the
point <m>(1,1)</m>, if the ant moves in the positive <m>x</m>-direction
while its <m>y</m>-coordinate stays fixed, the temperature increases at
a rate of about <m>8\,{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m> per foot.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> By definition <m>T_{xy} = (T_x)_y</m>, so we compute
<m>T_x</m> at two different values of <m>y</m> and then take a
difference quotient in <m>y</m>. We already have
<m>T_x(1,1) \approx 8</m>, and in the same way
<md>
T_x(1,1.5) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1.5) - T(1,1.5)}{0.5}
           = \frac{68-65}{0.5} = 6 .
</md>
Therefore
<md>
T_{xy}(1,1) \approx \frac{T_x(1,1.5) - T_x(1,1)}{1.5-1}
            = \frac{6-8}{0.5} = -4 .
</md>
The units are degrees Fahrenheit per foot per foot, i.e.
<m>{}^{\circ}\text{F}/\text{ft}^2</m>. This says that as we move in the
<m>y</m>-direction, the rate at which the temperature rises in the
<m>x</m>-direction is itself decreasing.
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> First we find the value of <m>t</m> at which the ant
is at <m>(1,1)</m>. We need
<md>
<mrow>1 - \frac{\ln(2t-3)}{8} = 1
\amp \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \ln(2t-3) = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad\Longrightarrow\quad 2t-3 = 1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad t = 2 ,</mrow>
</md>
and indeed <m>y = 2^2/4 = 1</m> at <m>t=2</m>, as required. Note that
<m>t=2</m> lies in the given interval <m>1.5 \lt t \lt 4</m>.
</p>

<p>
Next we differentiate the path:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = -\frac{1}{8}\cdot\frac{2}{2t-3}
              = -\frac{1}{4(2t-3)},
\amp \frac{dx}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = -\frac{1}{4} ,</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = \frac{2^t \ln 2}{4},
\amp \frac{dy}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = \frac{4\ln 2}{4} = \ln 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We also need <m>T_y(1,1)</m>, which we approximate from the table just
as before, stepping forward in <m>y</m>:
<md>
T_y(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1,1.5) - T(1,1)}{0.5} = \frac{65-70}{0.5}
         = -10 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now the chain rule gives
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dT}{dt} \amp = T_x\,\frac{dx}{dt} + T_y\,\frac{dy}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \approx (8)\left(-\frac14\right) + (-10)(\ln 2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -2 - 10\ln 2 \approx -8.93 .</mrow>
</md>
The units are degrees Fahrenheit per unit of <m>t</m> (per minute, if
<m>t</m> is measured in minutes). So the ant is cooling off at about
<m>8.9\,{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m> per unit of time. This makes sense: the
ant is moving slightly in the <m>-x</m> direction, which cools it a
little, and it is moving in the <m>+y</m> direction, which cools it a
lot. <xref ref="fig-q1-ant"/> shows the situation.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q1-ant">
<caption>
The nine data points of <xref ref="tab-q1-temps"/> and the ant's path.
At <m>t = 2</m> the ant is at <m>(1,1)</m> and its velocity is
<m>\left\langle -\tfrac14,\ \ln 2\right\rangle</m>. Temperatures fall
as we move up and to the left, so the ant is cooling.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<description>
<p>
A grid of nine labelled temperature readings on the x y plane, together
with the ant's curved path passing through the point one comma one, and
the velocity vector of the ant drawn at that point pointing up and
slightly to the left.
</p>
</description>
